\par\par \b \uc2  Harried euro finds moment's peace for Easter. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 821 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2000\par 04:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 19 (Reuters) - The harried euro found a moment's peace on Thursday as fast approaching holidays and the risk of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave bears second thoughts. \par \par \uc2  The euro inched up to $0.9411 by late morning in Tokyo having cratered at a new lifetime low around $0.9359 in New York. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market's immediate concern was the Bank of Japan given the Japanese authorities' repeated warnings about premature yen strength and the fact the euro was only a yen away from its record lows at 97.55 yen. \par \par \uc2  "There's a feeling that if the euro slides through 98.00 the BOJ could step in via dollar/yen, and that's even if the dollar's steady," said a European bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "Also Easter's coming and people are wary of being short." \par \par \uc2  The euro was hanging on at 98.46 yen having ended at 98.36 in New York, while the dollar was indeed holding steady at 104.65 yen against 104.75 in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Dealers reported good bids around 104.20 yen but heavy Japanese and U.S. offers from 105.15 upwards. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said would-be yen bulls were also given pause by speculation Moody's might soon announce the result of its review of Japan's debt rating. \par \par \uc2  This followed news Moody's managing director of sovereign risk Vincent Truglia will visit Tokyo next week and speak at the Foreign Correspondent's Club. \par \par \uc2  Japan's rating was placed under review for possible downgrade on February 17 and since such reviews typically take between two and three months, speculation has been heating up that the announcement could come at any time. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa also put the market on warning saying he had indicated to the rest of the G7 at last weekend's meeting that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone if necessary. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was less concerned about the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  given Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke's rejection of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a possibility earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  But still some fretted that with Europe on holiday on Friday and Monday, the resulting lack of liquidity may lead to the euro's slide becoming disorderly and the ECB might feel compelled to take a hand. \par \par \uc2  "Pretty much the whole world is on holiday on Friday bar Tokyo, so the market's going to be ultra-thin," said the dealer. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ has taken the opportunity to act on a Good Friday before and even the ECB might stir if the euro gaps lower." \par \par \uc2  But looking beyond the holiday, almost all dealers were convinced the euro was heading lower with 92 and 90 cents oft-mentioned targets. \par \par \uc2  Many reasons were cited including direct investment flows out of Europe, political uncertainty surrounding Italy and Austria, the relaxed attitude of the European authorities to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness and the seemingly irresistible attraction of U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  The last point was illustrated by the dollar's trade weighted index which has made decade highs on each of the last three days. \par \par \uc2  Even U.S. trade figures on Wednesday could not shake investor's confidence. The country's trade deficit expanded to a record $29.4 billion dollars in February, which was 57 percent up on the year before and meant the current account deficit was on the way to topping $400 billion this year. \par \par \uc2  And yet this also reflected how keen foreigners were to invest in the United States, a point noted by Federal Reserve Dallas President Robert McTeer. \par \par \uc2  While the U.S. economy was strong the deficit and capital inflows were sign of U.S. strength rather than weakness, he said. \par \par \uc2  He conceded that if foreigners began doubting the country's ability to sustain this economic strength, they may become more reluctant to fund the deficit and that could see the dollar fall. \par \par \uc2  However, McTeer stressed that he was optimistic about the continuing expansion. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0232 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   19 Apr.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab     0.9407   0.9403   +0.04    -6.58   1.0070\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab      104.68   104.76   -0.08    +2.42   102.21\par  Euro/yen\tab \tab      98.41    98.36   +0.05    -4.55   103.10\par  Dlr/swiss\tab \tab    1.6683   1.6690   -0.04    +4.91   1.5902\par  Stg/dlr\tab \tab      1.5797   1.5815   -0.11    -2.34   1.6176\par  Can/dlr\tab \tab      1.4754   1.4752   +0.01    +2.07   1.4455\par  Aus/dlr\tab \tab      0.5961   0.5952   +0.15    -9.17   0.6563\par  Euro/swiss\tab \tab   1.5698   1.5693   +0.03    -2.11   1.6036\par  Euro/stg\tab \tab     0.5953   0.5937   +0.27    -4.58   0.6239.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4k022hh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
