\par\par \b \uc2  Euro Could Plunge Vs Dollar If $0.9350 Level Cracked \b0\par\par\uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2000\par 23:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Jennifer Barrett and Robert Flint \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Easter isn't shaping up as much of a holiday for the euro. \par \par \uc2  With virtually all of Europe's financial markets closed Friday and Monday and most U.S. markets out Friday, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could face some very rough moments if pushed below its current all-time low against the dollar, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  The euro succumbed again to selling by speculators and long-term players midway through Thursday's New York session, slipping to a new low of $0.9355, just under its low of $0.9357 set Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Defense of various options at the $0.9350 and $0.9300 levels is credited in part for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's subsequent small bounce above its historical low versus the dollar. But if these defenses are breached in thin pre-holiday trading, the euro could face a fast and furious fall, perhaps to $0.9000 or even lower. Late Thursday the euro was trading at $0.9375, slightly weaker than $0.9404 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro also benefited from the dollar's gains against the yen, with each \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gaining more than a half-yen during the day, Dealers say the yen-selling was prompted by renewed talk of a possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen in the thin pre- or post-holiday sessions. \par \par \uc2  While the euro was able to stay at or near the 99-yen mark throughout the day, it could not hold off advances from other western \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro also fell to a new historical low against the pound mid-morning, trading as low as GBP0.5922. Late Thursday, the euro was trading not far from all-time lows against most of its major counterparts. \par \par \uc2  In these anemic pre-holiday conditions, even small orders can cause big ripples in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. But while the euro's movements may have been exaggerated, dealers say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not likely to reverse course against the dollar or pound when market players return next week. \par \par \uc2  "We've seen all kinds of people who were neutral to bullish, now looking to sell the euro," said Greg Anderson, financial economist at FleetBoston's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk. "The last holdouts are trying to square away their positions, not to hold on to them anymore." \par \par \uc2  Anderson said long-term investors are also throwing in the towel. "They're tired of getting hurt," he added. \par \par \uc2  Even the prospect of a 25 basis-point rate hike by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  next week is not likely to provide much relief to the beleaguered single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In part, that's because the euro-zone's main refinancing rates are already so low, at 3.50% compared to 6.00% in the U.S. and U.K. \par \par \uc2  Larry Hatheway, global head of strategy at Warburg Dillon Read in Connecticut, said the euro's long term downward trend against the dollar has largely been driven by the difference in long-term real rates of return between the two markets. \par \par \uc2  "It doesn't seem to me obvious that changing short-term interest rates is going to reverse investor flows," said Hatheway. \par \par \uc2  The ECB meets next Thursday to consider a rate hike, but the market seems to be divided on what action - if any - central bankers will take. \par \par \uc2  "The tone of the ECB is changing. Their efforts to talk the market higher and stabilize the euro have clearly picked up," said Hatheway. "The level of concern is there and the level of frustration has risen as well." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, ECB officials have indicated they do not plan on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and they do not want the euro's levels to determine\par any decisions they make concerning monetary policy.\par  -By Jennifer Barrett, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-4425\par   email:jennifer.barrett@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw4k020g7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
