\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Options - Euro/dlr vols firm as euro sinks. \b0\par\par\uc2 420 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2000\par 21:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, April 19 (Reuters) - Implied volatilities on major dollar pair options were mixed late Wednesday in quiet trading as the euro hovered over new historic lows. \par \par \uc2  "Euro vols have ticked up a bit because we have broken through the 94.00 cent figure in the spot. We have been down to the lowest levels we have ever seen in the euro," said an options trader at a major \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer. \par \par \uc2  Implied volatility on one-month euro/dollar pushed up to near 12.75/13.05 percent versus 12.45/12.65 late Tuesday. One-year euro/dollar implied volatility firmed to 12.2/12.4 percent Wednesday from 12.1/12.25 percent late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Accordingly, there was a slight shift in bias toward favouring euro puts. One-month euro/ $25-delta risk reversals were quoted at about 0.2 percent mid for puts, a switch from late yesterday when the one-month risk reversal was slightly favouring calls. \par \par \uc2  The euro dropped to a new record low of 93.59 cents against the dollar, easily breaking below the former low of 93.90 cents from February 28. \par \par \uc2  "The market is a bit nervous in this unchartered territory so vols popped up but it has been relatively quiet considering what's been going on," an options broker said. \par \par \uc2  Traders attributed the selling pressure in euro/dollar spot to this week's U.S. equity market recovery as well as doubts that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would act to stem the declines in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nasdaq Composite index\tab    rallied to test the midpoint of the sharp sell-off from the April 10 highs at 3,851 midmorning Wednesday, but fell into negative territory in afternoon dealings.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One-month volatility in dollar/yen was lower, near 13.3/13.6 percent, down from 13.9/14.2 percent late Tuesday as the underlying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was confined within a tight trading range.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen was slightly weaker, consolidating near 104.75 against the dollar Wednesday afternoon, as traders feared another round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One-year dollar/yen volatility was at about 14.1/14.3 percent Wednesday, down from 14.2/14/4 late Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One-month 25-delta risk reversals were favouring calls at 2.75 mid over puts, also softer than Tuesday afternoon levels of 2.9/3.2 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Chicago Derivatives Desk(312)408-8750\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par chicago.derivatives.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4j022et\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
