\par\par \b \uc2  Euro Hits New All-Time Low; Trades Under $0.9390 \b0\par\par\uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2000\par 17:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)-  Pressured by heavy selling in the European and early New York sessions, Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped to a new all-time low against the dollar late Wednesday morning in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to $0.9375 just after 11:00 a.m. EDT (1500 GMT) after stop-loss sales below the 94-cent mark sent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbling below its old historical low of $0.9390 from Feb. 28, say dealers. \par \par \uc2  "There was nothing as usual supporting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , unfortunately," said a New York-based trader. "We thought it would hold up, but there was a lot of pressure." \par \par \uc2  "It's basically a continuation of the pattern we've seen, with momentum traders pushing it down and long-term stop losses at $0.9390. The market just went for the easy push and it went to $0.9375," said Joe Francomano, strategic trader at Erste Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro quickly popped back up above 94 cents before beginning a more gradual slide down again. \par \par \uc2  Around noon EDT (1600 GMT), the euro is trading just above $0.9350 \par \par \uc2  , below $0.9463 at the beginning of the New York session and $0.9473 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Francomano said he expects the euro to trade between $0.9350 and $0.9450 for the rest of the day. \par \par \uc2  Michael McGuinness, senior director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at American Express in New York, cited one reason in particular for the euro's failure to appreciate. While the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is popular as a funding source, the euro-zone region isn't so attractive as a place to invest. \par \par \uc2  Foreign companies and investors like the low interest rates in the region. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has set refinancing rates at 3.50%, compared with 6.0% rates in the U.S. and U.K. \par \par \uc2  But after borrowing money in euros, the proceeds are often invested abroad, so the euros are converted to the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . High inflation among the 11 countries that make up the euro zone and better returns in the U.S. and Asian stock markets have discouraged investors from putting their money into Europe. \par \par \uc2  For the euro to appreciate, McGuinness said, there would have to be less demand in issuing debt in the euro. Instead, he said the number of issues in euros is increasing. \par \par \uc2  That isn't likely to change even if the ECB raises the region's main refinancing rate when it meets next Thursday, particularly as the Federal Reserve is expected to raise U.S. interest rates by at least 25 basis points at its May meeting. \par \par \uc2  The euro's best hope for appreciation may actually come from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen strengthens too much. But while the euro hit new all-time lows against the pound and the dollar Wednesday, it remains about a yen above its historical low against the yen of Y97.50, according to a trader. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Around noon EDT (1600 GMT), the euro is buying Y98.26, down from\par Y98.77 earlier and Y99.23 late Tuesday in New York.\par    -By Jennifer Barrett and Tom Barkley; Dow Jones Newswires;\par 201-938-4384; jennifer.barrett@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw4j0211a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
