\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Higher Vs Yen; Focus On US, Japan Stocks \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 965 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2000\par 07:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar edged higher against the yen in Tokyo Tuesday, amid moderate buying by market players reassured by Monday's rebound in U.S. stock prices and Tuesday's continued selloff of Japanese shares. \par \par \uc2  Concerns that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be poised to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets also bolstered the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro, however, fell against the dollar and the yen as the euro-zone stock markets declined Monday and euro-supportive remarks from European finance and monetary officials had a negligible impact. \par \par \uc2  After suffering its worst point drop in a single day, the Dow Jones Industrial Average rebounded strongly late Monday, leaping 276.75, or 2.69%, to 10582.51. The Nasdaq Composite Index rallied sharply, rising 217.67, or 6.55%, to 3538.96, recording its biggest single-day point gain ever. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar-yen got a boost from the substantial rebound in U.S. stock prices overnight," said Ken Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Japanese stocks, on the other hand, continued their selloff, which gave further support to the dollar. As of 0520 GMT, the Nikkei index of 225 was down 453.98 points at 18554.66, after it plunged 1426.04 points Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also supported by heightened concern that the BOJ might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar rose more than a yen to an intraday high of Y105.12 when the same major Japanese bank that bought a large quantity of dollars Monday morning repeated its performance Tuesday, sparking familiar fears. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese bank that bought dollar-yen yesterday reappeared today to buy dollar-yen, triggering rumors that the BOJ was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as that bank is frequently used as an agent of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Takeo Suzuki, assistant \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Trust and Banking. \par \par \uc2  When interbank players discovered the facts behind the rumors, they sold the dollar to the low-Y104s before short-covering brought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back up to the mid-Y104s. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, analysts say the U.S. equity markets - and by extension the dollar - are not out of the woods yet. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. policy mix, in particular the Federal Reserve's monetary policy, will continue to weigh on the (equity) markets. As long as the Fed continues to raise rates, the markets are at risk," Landon said. He forecasts that the dollar could test the Y102.50 level this week, especially if the U.S. equity markets falter again. \par \par \uc2  Suzuki also expects a lower dollar-yen this week, subject to U.S. stock market weakness. "I think the dollar's downside should be limited to Y103.00 even if U.S. stock prices plummet again. The dollar's upside should be capped at Y106.00." \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT (1:15 a.m. EDT), the dollar was quoted at Y104.64, above Y104.37 late Monday in New York. The dollar was at Y103.95 late Monday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $0.9498, lower than $0.9524 late Monday in New York. The euro was at $0.9650 in Tokyo Monday. \par \par \uc2  The euro lost ground against both the dollar and the yen as sentiment toward the euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soured again, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. stock markets recovered, the euro-zone stock markets dropped, and the euro didn't react positively to friendly comments from European government officials," said Shuji Takano, assistant \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at ABN AMRO Bank. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Vice President Christian Noyer on Monday echoed comments from other ECB officials, saying the euro's external value doesn't yet reflect the "ongoing improvement in the domestic fundamentals of the euro-area economy." \par \par \uc2  Noyer also observed that "the value of the euro should be higher, given the underlying strength of the euro zone, and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be lower." But his remarks fell on deaf market ears. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is ignoring such remarks from European officials as well as economic indicators," Landon said. \par \par \uc2  Germany's Ifo business climate index for March is scheduled to be released at 0830 GMT. Market consensus is for a reading of 101.5, compared with February's reading of 100.9. \par \par \uc2  But even strong data isn't likely to give the euro more than a short-term lift. \par \par \uc2  "While a (strong) Ifo might give the euro a short blip higher, the impact won't be a large one and the (euro's) trend will still be downward," Landon said. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, even if the U.S. stock markets decline again, the euro is expected to run into further selling pressure in the coming days, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese investors and exporters believe there's more downside risk for the euro-yen, as they think European government officials are indifferent to the euro's weakness. As such, we might see euro-yen sales later today," Takano said. \par \par \uc2  Landon predicted the euro could test its all-time low of $0.9390 against the dollar and of Y97.55 against the yen this week. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y99.39, down from Y99.61 in New York late Monday. The euro was at Y100.31 in Tokyo late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.5790, down from $1.5812 in New York and lower than $1.5905 in Tokyo Monday. \par \par \uc2  -By Ron Harui; 813-5255-2942; ron.harui@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates which accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture which is updated hourly. \par \par \uc2  Subscribers can find this table: \par \par \uc2  -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087. \par \par \uc2  -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX \par \par \uc2  -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT \par \par \uc2  -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw4i020k0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
