\par\par \b \uc2  Yen and Swiss franc to profit as stocks gyrate. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 741 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2000\par 17:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 17 (Reuters) - Wall Street stocks' dizzying gyrations lately favour \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which have the natural advantage of large current account surpluses - namely the yen and the Swiss franc, analysts said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq composite, the symbol of America's technology-driven "new economy", bounced nearly four percent on Monday after tumbling almost 10 percent on Friday, while the blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average also bounced more than one percent after losing five percent on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The swings will likely make international investors more inclined to keep their money at home - at a time when robust consumption in America is keeping U.S. current account deficit at record levels, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "It's the very familiar argument about the fact that the U.S. needs to import about $1 billion of capital per day for the curreny account imbalance," said Andrew Snowball, international economist at Julius Baer Investments in London. \par \par \uc2  Analysts noted that the United States had been able to run record deficit for years, due in part to its stock market's strength which attracted foreign investment. But once such capital infusion dried up, the dollar would have to depreciate. \par \par \uc2  "So far the market has ignored the current account deficit. But now you're going to start seeing it come into focus," said Ryan Shea, international economist at Bank One in London. \par \par \uc2  On the flipside, countries with large current account surpluses typically see their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate when investors keep more of their money at home. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese have a large current account surplus, the Swiss have a large surplus and even Europe (euro zone) has a surplus," Shea said. \par \par \uc2  EURO YET TO GAIN SAFE-HAVEN STATUS \par \par \uc2  Of those three \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said the euro - launched in January 1999 - had yet to grow into a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given its inability to pull itself out of its life-long decline despite improving euro zone economic data. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the euro would really benefit from this," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez in London. "Unless we start seeing some significant signs the U.S. economy itself is deteriorating, it's unlikely to have a real beneficial impact on the euro." \par \par \uc2  The yen's advance on Monday was slowed partly by wariness that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its strength. \par \par \uc2  But traders said fear of a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had all but vanished after after the Group of Seven industrial powers did not mention their concern about the economic impact of yen strength at their weekend meeting. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, much of the actual fund flows in recent sessions were seen to have been domestic, with investors bailing out of once high-flying technology stocks into defensive "old economy" stocks and into U.S. Treasury securities. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment, the beneficiaries have been the bond markets," Kotecha at Credit Agricole said. \par \par \uc2  That is seen as one reason the euro had been unable to make significant gains against the dollar. The euro remained near the middle of recent tight ranges near $0.96 - not very far from record lows near $0.94 hit in February. \par \par \uc2  But Snowball at Julius Baer said further Wall Street losses could eventually lead to a euro rebound. \par \par \uc2  "You could have falling stocks markets and the Fed (U.S. Federal Reserve) continuing to hike rates, and both of those factors pushing the economy into a harder landing," he added. "Then you'll see both the direct effect of capital flight from the U.S. and the economic effect of a slower economy." \par \par \uc2  So far, the Nasdaq composite has lost about a third of its value from a dizzying record high of 5132.52 set on March 10 and the Dow has fallen 13 percent from its peaks hit in January. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts were sceptical about whether stock prices would be a major factor for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all, particularly as losses in the broader market was not as severe. \par \par \uc2  "Econometrics studies show, to get any kind of a real economy response, you need a 20-percent collapse across all equities and it has to be sustained - neither of which is true at the moment," said Peter von Maydell, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in London. \par \par \uc2  "Trading \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  off equities is a recipe for losing money," he quipped. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4h025ne\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
