\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr eases,nervous as US stock slide overshadows G7. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ayumi Moriyama \par \uc2 757 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2000\par 03:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 16 (Reuters) - The dollar eased from early highs but traded nervously against the yen early on Monday in Tokyo as a downdraft in share prices that started on Wall Street last week overshadowed the bland outcome of a G7 meeting at the weekend. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jumped to 105.50 yen in early trade amid rumours the Bank of Japan was buying dollars soon after the opening of the Tokyo stock market, when the benchmark Nikkei average slid five percent after the carnage in U.S. share prices on Friday. \par \par \uc2  During morning trade the dollar eased from its early high to 105.00/05 yen, but was above Friday's New York close of 104.75. \par \par \uc2  The euro which firmed a bit against the dollar due in part to the nosedive in U.S. shares, stood at $0.9644/49 compared with $0.9619 in late New York trade. \par \par \uc2  "The (Group of Seven meeting's) failure to mention concern about yen strength is putting some downward pressure on the dollar, but the main concern is the outlook for the stock market and inflationary fears (in the United States)," said Yasuharu Tsuru, manager of the international treasury division at Mitsubishi Trust & Banking. \par \par \uc2  The catalyst for the U.S. stock dive was a stronger-than-expected Consumer Price Index for March that showed inflation at the consumer level rose at a much faster rate than anticipated. \par \par \uc2  The communique released after Saturday's meeting of finance ministers and central bankers from the G7 industrialised nations in Washington did not refer explicitly to the yen, euro or dollar, saying instead that "exchange rates among the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect economic fundamentals". \par \par \uc2  But dealers agreed the dollar's fate seemed to depend on what foreign investors decide to do with their Japanese assets as shares are dragged lower by the Dow Jones industrial average's largest one-day point loss ever and the Nasdaq composite index's dip by a record 355.49 points. \par \par \uc2  "We need to see if foreign investors decide to pull out their money from Japanese stocks. If they do, this will be a dollar-buying factor," said a Japanese city bank trader. \par \par \uc2  Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said they expected there may be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) should players test the downside to 103.00 yen, close to levels where Japanese authorities last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on April 3. \par \par \uc2  In an attempt to soothe market nerves, senior Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi on Monday said there was no change in Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance after the G7 meeting, even though its communique did not mention concerns about yen strength as had previous statements in January and September. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said that, despite the missing mention of the yen, a phrase regarding Japan's determination to keep short-term interest rates as close to zero as possible may be taken as a dollar-supportive factor. \par \par \uc2  "The line about the zero-rate policy is likely to support the dollar as it gives the impression that the BOJ will maintain its policy at least in the following three months or so," another city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The communique states: "The Japanese authorities decided to continue, in the context of their zero interest-rate policy, to provide ample liquidity to ensure that deflationary concerns are dispelled." \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami shocked the markets last week when he displayed impatience with the zero-rate policy, stating that the markets were not wrong to think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was thinking about tightening monetary policy. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0106 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls \par \par \uc2  14 Apr. \par \par \uc2  ----------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2  Euro/dlr   0.9649   0.9619   +0.31    -4.18   1.0070 \par \par \uc2  Dlr/yen    104.80   104.75   +0.05    +2.53   102.21 \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen   101.08   100.76   +0.32    -1.96   103.10 \par \par \uc2  Dlr/swiss  1.6259   1.6310   -0.31    +2.25   1.5902 \par \par \uc2  Stg/dlr    1.5880   1.5877   +0.02    -1.83   1.6176 \par \par \uc2  Can/dlr    1.4762   1.4763   -0.01    +2.12   1.4455 \par \par \uc2  Aus/dlr    0.6033   0.5973   +1.00    -8.08   0.6563 \par \par \uc2  Euro/swiss 1.5690   1.5702   -0.08    -2.16   1.6036 \par \par \uc2  Euro/stg   0.6060   0.6033   +0.45    -2.87   0.6239. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4h024eq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
