\par\par \b \uc2  Markets see little hope of G7 help on euro, yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elizabeth Lazarowitz \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2000\par 21:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, April 13 (Reuters) - Contradictory statements by finance leaders from top industrial nations on possible cures for the ailing euro and aggressive yen leave \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers expecting a bland outcome to this weekend's G7 meeting. \par \par \uc2  For a brief time, dealers had high hopes that finance officials from the world's seven richest nations, meeting in Washington on Saturday, would issue strong statements expressing their concerns about the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But after a week of explosive and often contradictory official comments from around the world, analysts said the most the markets can expect is another plain vanilla statement that fails to push any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of its well worn path. \par \par \uc2  "The mood surrounding the G7 meeting is rapidly shifting from expectations there might be some joint statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to one where it will be a major victory if the biggest differences can be contained behind closed doors," said Larry Hatheway, global head of strategy at Warburg Dillon Read. \par \par \uc2  If indeed the G7 offers nothing more than familiar phrases of concern about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' movements, the euro, already under pressure because the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to raise rates on Thursday, stands to lose the most. \par \par \uc2  The common European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could swiftly hit support near 94.50 cents and then plough on to fresh life-time lows under 93.90 cents, said dealers after the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turned lower again on Thursday, trading near 95.34 cents. \par \par \uc2  The yen, for its part, is ready to step higher, forcing the dollar off its current 105.78 yen level back toward recent lows near 103 yen. \par \par \uc2  The only obstacle in its path may be the specter of more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  some 17 times since last summer to prevent excessive yen strength from choking off economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "Basically we can probably roll back the tape to the last G7 meeting and react the same way we did back then," said Ralph DelZenero, vice president at Banc One before the meeting. \par \par \uc2  "The market place is set up for a potential rally for the yen," said Mike Malpede, senior analyst at Refco Group. \par \par \uc2  The market's blase attitude stands in sharp contrast to earlier speculation that Japan, with help from France, would press their five partner nations to finally speak out freely about the dangers of the fast-falling euro. \par \par \uc2  But Tokyo itself seems to have quashed that possibility when its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor on Wednesday suggested that interest rates may soon begin to rise from ground zero, whetting investors' appetite for more yen. \par \par \uc2  Masaru Hayami also said he did not expect the G7 to dwell on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues or discuss specific \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  To turn back the irritation some of its partner nations are sure to be feeling now, Japan would need to reassure other ministers that their ultra-easy monetary policy, aimed at jump-starting recovery, will be kept for some time. \par \par \uc2  But that will be tough, especially because dealers feel Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers has signalled his irritation with the sluggish pace of reforms in Japan and Europe. \par \par \uc2  Summers drove home his point at a briefing on Thursday, telling reporters that Japan knows reforms are urgently needed to sustain domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  The chances for the euro to get an officially sanctioned boost were also slim in light of the comments made to date. \par \par \uc2  Belgium's Finance Minister Didier Reynders said on Wednesday that the 11-nation euro zone did not want a statement on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness. The next day, however, Dutchman Wim Duisenberg, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, said the euro, yen and dollar will be discussed on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4d02gvu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
