\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  dealers bet euro to get G7 mention. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Jucca \par \uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2000\par 17:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 11 (Reuters) - The yen's broad retreat in recent weeks could see policy-makers from the Group of Seven industrial nations focus on euro weakness rather than yen strength when they meet this weekend, analysts said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Helped by the most recent round of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen-curbing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on April 3, the yen has shed five percent against the dollar in less than two weeks and about six percent from record peaks it hit against the euro on March 31. \par \par \uc2  While Japan has driven the yen away from the key 100 per dollar level, euro/yen is still at levels which are as painful for Japanese exporters as for those Japanese investors who bought euro-denominated assets in the past year. \par \par \uc2  This, together with remarks made on Tuesday by Japanese officials, have got traders thinking the G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors could issue a statement spelling out concern about persistent euro weakness at their Washington talks. \par \par \uc2  "The level of the yen against the euro is becoming the main concern for the Japanese," said James Shugg, international economist at Westpac in London. \par \par \uc2  "The fact the yen has been softening over the last week shows people anticipate something happening at the weekend. It's absolutely a promise the euro is going to be on the agenda this weekend and the Japanese might push for a statement on the euro." \par \par \uc2  The G7 consists of the United States, Japan, Germany, France, Britain, Italy, and Canada. \par \par \uc2  JAPANESE REMARKS INCREASE G7 WARINESS After their last meeting in January, the G7 issued a statement in which they said they shared Japan's concern against the strength of the yen. However, they made no specific reference to the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Also, European Union ministers meeting in Lisbon last week said they would not seek a G7 euro supportive statement. \par \par \uc2  But talk the euro could get a mention sprung back into dealers' minds when Japanese Economic Planning Minister Taichi Sakaiya said on Tuesday the G7 was likely to discuss the euro. \par \par \uc2  Furthermore, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers scaled back their expectations of how strongly the G7 would express concern about past yen firmness after Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said Japan had nothing particular it wanted to press at the meeting. \par \par \uc2  "There is speculation in the market that the G7 might say something on the euro this time given the Japanese keep talking about it," said Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Rabobank in London. \par \par \uc2  "If we do get something on the yen is going to be about euro/yen rather than dollar/yen. The dollar is trading around 107 yen and the Japanese seem to be quite happy about it." \par \par \uc2  Still, some analysts said concern about the export-damaging impact of yen strength was still paramount for the Japanese authorities, who have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least four times so far this year to curb its bull run. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, any G7 mention of the euro was unlikely to go beyond a reiteration of European official's mantra that the euro had the potential to appreciate, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "There is some speculation in the market that the we might get mention of the euro in the G7 communique," but any reference is likely to be rather oblique," said Michael Lewis, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  With \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers increasingly expecting a more explicit statement on the euro at the upcoming G7 meeting, analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers could be disappointed if the communique turned to be bland. \par \par \uc2  The risks on the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were seen on the downside and it was expected to quickly giving back gains it had managed to rack up this week if the G7 failed to mention it. \par \par \uc2  "Even if they said something, any reaction in euro would only last half a day," said Daivd Brown, chief economist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  "In the old days the G7 really used to move the market around. But those days are passed and gone. All they come out now is a continuation of the status quo, fairly mediocre statements that really aren't going to make an awful lot of difference to the price action for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets." \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets 0171 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4b02i6z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
