\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Japan reiterates resolve to tame yen as G7 nears. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoko Nishikawa \par \uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2000\par 08:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 11 (Reuters) - Japan is determined to fend off a rapid rise in the yen as it prepares for a G7 meeting this weekend, still worried about a tentative economic recovery, Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But he played down Japan's eagerness to urge other members of the Group of Seven industrial nations to express shared concern about the yen's strength when their finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs meet in Washington on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  "The meeting will discuss this issue, but we don't intend to be the ones that bring it up," Miyazawa told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  The financial markets widely expect the G7 nations will reiterate their shared concerns about a strong yen, as they did at meetings in January and September. The absence of any reference to such concerns would likely send the yen higher. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have long expressed worries that the yen's strength since last summer threatens to choke off a recovery from the nation's worst postwar slump, and Miyazawa indicated those concerns remain despite signs of gradual improvement in the economy in recent months. \par \par \uc2  "We have been taking action in line with our stance that a rapid yen rise is problematic. That stance remains unchanged," he said. \par \par \uc2  G7 CONCERN BOLSTERS \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN CURRENCY INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Market sources say the G7's signals of agreement with Japan's yen worries have bolstered the effectiveness of Japanese authorities' repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent months, which have helped keep the dollar above 100 yen. \par \par \uc2  Strength in the yen inflames deflationary pressures in Japan, by making imports less expensive, and whittles down the yen value of Japanese companies' overseas earnings. \par \par \uc2  Although Miyazawa said there were no issues that Japan would actively lobby for at the G7 meeting, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets still expect an expression of shared concern. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood near 106.60 yen on Tuesday afternoon in Tokyo, up slightly from its New York close of around 106.40 yen on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Former Vice Finance Minister Kosuke Nakahira told Reuters on Tuesday that there would be less urgency now for G7 to mention concerns over the yen, given current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market conditions. \par \par \uc2  The dollar/yen has been in a range of 102-112 yen since the January G7 meeting, with the yen keeping upward momentum, but a recent Bank of Japan survey found major manufacturers have adjusted their budgeted average exchange rate to around 104 yen for the year that began on April 1, compared with 112 yen for the previous year. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa also said Japan will tell its G7 partners that its economic prospects have brightened, with a recovery now visible on the horizon although the initial pickup will be gradual and unemployment remains a problem. \par \par \uc2  He said he would also explain that corporate spending, the second-largest component of the economy after personal consumption, has improved and that the government is determined to avoid sharp increases in bond issuance in the years ahead. \par \par \uc2  Japan's government debt issuance has surged in recent years to finance several economic stimulus packages, generating a bloated fiscal deficit that poses a headache for Japanese policy-makers. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4b02gl1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
