\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Dollar Seen Rangebound Ahead Of G7 Meeting \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Evans \par \uc2 851 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2000\par 00:00\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar is likely to remain on hold against the yen and the euro this week, pending the outcome of the meeting of finance ministers from the world's leading industrialized nations April 15 in Washington. \par \par \uc2  And it's likely that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  players could use a break after the tumultuous events of last week, which began with a massive salvo of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cool the yen, segued into several days of whipsaw trading on the meltdown of world stock markets, and concluded with the dollar clinging to extremely tight ranges against its major counterparts. \par \par \uc2  Many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  players are waiting for the G7 meeting - which will take place on the sidelines of the International Monetary Fund and World Bank Spring meetings - to attack the problem of the weak euro and the strong yen in more explicit terms than at past gatherings in January and last September. \par \par \uc2  If signs emerge of a concerted effort to weaken the yen and boost the euro, some say the euro could push back into the $0.9700 to $0.9800 band that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has tried and failed to surmount three times since the beginning of March. \par \par \uc2  A dose of such rhetoric could also push the dollar back toward the Y108 area, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers say. \par \par \uc2  But lacking a clear sense yet of a ready consensus to cheapen the yen and bolster the euro, a more likely scenario for this week is a dollar/euro rate continuing to oscillate between $0.9500 and $0.9700, with dollar/yen moving between Y104 and Y106.5, according to many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders. \par \par \uc2  Before the attention is fully focused on Washington, a key event for the euro will be Thursday's policy-setting meeting of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at which many expect the euro-zone \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise its benchmark refinancing rate a quarter percentage point to 3.75%. \par \par \uc2  Absent a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -supportive rate hike, analysts say a euro plunge below $0.9500 to new all-time lows versus the dollar is possible. But even higher rates are likely to be of limited benefit to the euro, which continues to struggle despite good euro-zone economic fundamentals and more efforts last week by ECB officials to talk up its potential for appreciation. \par \par \uc2  To many, the euro's upside against the dollar will likely remain limited until European enthusiasm for U.S. financial assets subsides on a more lasting basis, as last week's events demonstrated. \par \par \uc2  "When equity markets swooned last week, that was the one time that the euro showed a breath of life," said senior economist Avery Shenfeld of CIBC World Markets in Toronto. "But it didn't last, and that does tell us that the euro isn't going anywhere as long as U.S. equity markets are stable to rising." \par \par \uc2  With the specter of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  still hanging over the market after it's estimated $10 billion yen-selling binge last Monday, the dollar isn't likely to move far either against the yen until the Washington monetary summit concludes. But most analysts believe current indications still point to a stronger yen. \par \par \uc2  This week, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  players will be watching to see if Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party will seek to move up the date for Japan's next general election in a bid to win a sympathy vote after former Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi suffered a crippling stroke over a week ago. \par \par \uc2  New Japanese Prime Minister Yoshiro Mori is a sure bet to maintain the fiscally stimulative policies of his stricken predecessor, and analysts say perceptions of accelerated fiscal stimulus, coupled with recent indications of a modest Japanese economic recovery, point to an eventual test of the Y100 level for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We'll learn this week how quickly elections accelerate fiscal spending in the short term, which is a short-term yen positive," said James Conklin, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers Inc. in New York. "As the political situation in Japan clarifies, I think the balance of risk is for a new run at Y100 - we were beginning to make a run at it when the prime minister had a stroke, and that took us back to Y104-Y105." \par \par \uc2  Another hot topic for the yen this week and further into April is how over $100 billion of maturing deposits this month from Japan's postal savings system will be re-invested, whether in Japan or in search of higher-yielding instruments offshore. \par \par \uc2  That will be a "huge determinant" of the yen's value going forward, said Andrew Busch, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the Bank of Montreal in Chicago. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Where that money ends up is obviously the object of a great deal of\par speculation," Busch said. "You'd think those investors are looking to\par move some of that money overseas, but on the other hand equities are\par performing exceptionally well in Japan, and probably will continue to do\par so."\par  -By Paul Evans, Dow Jones Newswires; (416) 306-2022\par - paulr.evans@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1141 4137 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020010805dw49001qx\par }\page 