\par\par \b \uc2  Japan external reserves rise to $305.51 bln in Mar. \b0\par\par\uc2 185 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2000\par 09:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 3 (Reuters) - Japan's external reserves rose to a record high of $305.512 billion at the end of March, up $11.045 billion from a month earlier, the Finance Ministry said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  External reserves at the end of February totalled $294.467 billion, up $1.313 billion from the previous month. \par \par \uc2  March was the fourth consecutive month in which the figure hit a record high. \par \par \uc2  The reserves number is watched by the market for clues on the extent of Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Market sources said external reserves rose in March mainly due to a series \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in which the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) sold yen for dollars and euros. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ acts as an agent for the Finance Ministry and conducts actual yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ was detected selling yen for dollars and euros on March 8. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also sold yen for dollars on March 15. ((Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw43034u6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
