\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Rubber-TOCOM pulls up mkt, Indonesia attracts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nao Nakanishi \par \uc2 480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2000\par 08:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, April 3 (Reuters) - Southeast Asian physical rubber prices are likely to rise further, led by futures prices in Tokyo in the wake of speculative buying after Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of dealers are coming in to pick up SIR20," said a trader. "It's still worthwhile that you buy that (SIR20) and sell RSS3 or Japan. They're nice differentials." \par \par \uc2  Traders said buyers were turning to Indonesia because SIR \par \par \uc2   was the cheapest, with offers seen at 29    U.S. cents/lb for \par \par \uc2   July and 30 cents for August, FOB Palembang. \par \par \uc2     There was talk of tightening raw materials supply in \par \par \uc2  Indonesia, but aggressive selling capped price increases over the past week. \par \par \uc2  The rebound in prices for Thai RSS3 and Malaysian SMR20 followed a slide over the past several weeks, they said. \par \par \uc2  "There's a lot of demand and a lot of supply," said another trader, referring to Indonesia. "But now we have wintering in Indonesia. Although rubber is traded low, supply is getting tight." \par \par \uc2  In hopes of further price increases, suppliers in Thailand seemed to be holding back stocks, pushing up RSS3 prices to 67-68 U.S. cents/kg for May and 69 cents for June shipments, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Some traders said Malaysian SMR20 was also firm at around 67 cents/kg as rains cut raw material supplies to 25-30 percent of usual levels. All eyes were on TOCOM, which bounced strongly and pulled up physical rubber prices early on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The move came amid Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's gains against the dollar, which triggered buying of yen-denominated rubber contracts by individual speculators, traders said. \par \par \uc2  They were watching whether large consumers, such as tyre makers, would place sizable orders following their return to the market last week after an absence since the Lunar New Year holidays in February. \par \par \uc2  Many were seen covered for nearby positions, including some needs for August and September, but some last week were buying distant months as far as the first quarter of next year. \par \par \uc2  Traders said this helped rubber prices to turnaround, which many described as a technical correction after a long decline. \par \par \uc2  "A lot depends on whether consumers are getting aggressive," said a third trader. "Buyers have been buying really forward months. They are not really aggressive...Some consumers are picking up here and there next year January and March." \par \par \uc2  The first trader added: "They don't seem to be in particular hurry at moment...There might be a bit of a run, but I don't think it will last too long because of the sheer weight of rubber around. We'll see proper supply after a while." \par \par \uc2  ((Singapore newsroom 65-870-3080, fax 65-776-8112, singapore.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw43033v9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
