\par\par \b \uc2  REPEAT-UPDATE 2-Tankan shows recovery fragile, Japan sells yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2000\par 07:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 3 (Reuters) - Big Japanese companies were more optimistic on business conditions for the fifth quarter in a row, boosting hopes that the economy is pulling out of its worst slide in half a century, a key survey showed on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's quarterly "tankan" survey of business sentiment also showed that major firms largely expect to end a two year period of slashing capital investment, confirming an improvement in the second-biggest engine of the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  The tankan figures came in largely as expected, but economists cautioned that in an economic expansion, the survey's results usually beat expectations - meaning this nascent recovery looks particularly fragile. \par \par \uc2  "This is a tankan which is probably at its highest point, and as we go down into the fiscal year we may see these numbers being revised downwards," said Noriko hama, research director at Mitsubishi Research Institute. "This is probably the zenith." \par \par \uc2  The authorities, meanwhile, were taking no chances, selling yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stop a surge in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from derailing the recovery. \par \par \uc2  TANKAN HITS EXPECTATIONS \par \par \uc2  The tankan sentiment index for major manufacturers came in at minus nine, up from the minus 17 reading of the December tankan. \par \par \uc2  This was exactly what the companies had forecast for the index in the previous tankan and roughly matched expectations of minus 10 in a Reuters poll of economists last week. \par \par \uc2  All 22 economists in the Reuters survey had forecast improvement in the main index due to booming demand for information technology products and a recovery in exports to Asian markets. \par \par \uc2  Large manufacturers forecast a June index reading of minus five - showing continued modest improvement but meaning pessimists would still outnumber optimists. \par \par \uc2  The index is the difference between the percentage of companies reporting favourable business conditions and that saying conditions are unfavourable. \par \par \uc2  The tankan is a fervently watched bellwether of the world's second-biggest economy, which slid back into recession late last year on stubbornly weak personal spending but which at the same time has seen industrial output and corporate profits blossom. \par \par \uc2  Large companies in the tankan said they expected to reduce spending on plant and equipment by 0.6 percent in the business year that began on Saturday, after slashing such investment for two years. \par \par \uc2  The Reuters poll had forecast that capital spending, the second-biggest chunk of the economy, would roughly hit bottom, with a median forecast for a 1.0 percent decline. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has made clear that improvement in capital spending will be key to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision on when to end its 13-month-old policy of driving short-term interest rates to zero to stave off deflation and bolster the fragile economic improvement. \par \par \uc2  But the missing link to a durable Japanese recovery is a rebound in consumption, which remains elusive amid job worries as companies undertake painful restructuring. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  BOOSTS DOLLAR AGAINST YEN \par \par \uc2  Another perennial concern is the strong yen, which erodes the value of Japanese companies' overseas earnings in yen terms and inflames deflationary pressure on the economy. \par \par \uc2  After the dollar plunged more than three yen on Friday to within a whisker of 102.00 yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push the yen back down, bumping the dollar back above 104 yen in midmorning Tokyo trade. \par \par \uc2  "We have acted in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said senior Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi, speaking shortly after Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa pronounced Friday's yen surge as too abrupt. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw43033sn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
