\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-BOJ rescues dollar from slide but yen bullish. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2000\par 04:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 3 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  reined in the rising yen on Monday, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy dollars for yen, but the yen remained on an upward trend amid rising optimism about Japan's economy, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Early rounds of BOJ yen-selling quickly lifted the dollar above 104 yen from its early low of 102.82 yen, but its advance slowed as it approached 105 yen. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has been taking steps to keep the yen from rising sharply on perceptions that a steep rise could threaten Japan's still fragile economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The head of the Finance Ministry's International Bureau, Zembei Mizoguchi, confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "Looking at the way the dollar has jumped, it seems that the BOJ initially stepped in from around the low end of the 103 to 104 yen range," a commercial bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The dealer said the dollar climbed in large strides from around 103.30 yen, seen the point where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have initially stepped in to buy dollars for yen in the morning shortly after it released its quarterly "tankan" survey of corporate sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ was also detected to have bought dollars intermittently above 104 yen, sending the dollar as high as 105.30 yen, bankers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar suffered its latest setback on Friday, tumbling to a three-month low of 102.03 yen in overseas markets as speculative yen-buying grew after the euro broke below a psychologically significant support point of 100 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Speculative yen-buying intensified, reflecting brighter prospects for the Japanese economy and given the euro's slump. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dipped to a lifetime low of 97.55 yen on Friday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said, however, that the BOJ had not been detected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the euro on Monday morning. \par \par \uc2  Rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the dollar/yen rate also lifted the euro decisively above 100 yen. The euro stood at 100.24/37 yen at 0208 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "The market was very eager to sell dollars aggressively before the tankan data, but many operators became very cautious after seeing the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a Japanese city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "But that stance should only last for a short time," he added. "The market will be looking for chances to push down the dollar below 100 yen as we continued to see good signs from tankan data." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's tankan survey released on Monday showed that big Japanese companies believe business conditions have improved in the past three months, boosting hopes that the economy is pulling out of its worst slide in half a century. \par \par \uc2  The tankan sentiment index for major manufacturers came in at minus nine, up from the minus 17 reading in the December tankan. \par \par \uc2  The index is the difference between the percentage of companies reporting favourable business conditions and that saying conditions are unfavourable. \par \par \uc2  The tankan is a fervently watched bellwether of the world's second-biggest economy, which slid back into recession late last year on stubbornly weak personal spending but which at the same time has seen industrial output and corporate profits blossom. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Japanese life insurers were also detected buying dollars for yen, but the dollar's recovery could be temporary as many exporters and profit-takers were still eager to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 105 yen. \par \par \uc2  At 0208 GMT, the dollar stood at 105.12/15 yen compared with a U.S. close of 102.67/77 yen on Friday. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw43030cx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
