\par\par \b \uc2  Snapshot of euro performance as ECB meets-March 30. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,025 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 10:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 29 (Reuters) - The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) holds its fortnightly policy-making meeting on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to leave interest rates unchanged. It last raised rates on March 16, lifting its refinancing rate by a quarter percentage point to 3.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  The following is a snapshot of the euro's performance to date and a selection of the most recent comments by ECB and other European officials on the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  EURO MOVES TO DATE: \par \par \uc2  ** The euro has fallen 1.8 percent against the dollar since the ECB's last policy meeting on March 16, and was at $0.9536/40 at 0744 GMT on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  ** The euro fell to a record low of $0.9390 on February 28, down 20 percent from levels it began trading at after its January, 1999 launch. \par \par \uc2  ** The euro fell to record lows near 100 yen on Wednesday, down 24.5 percent since its launch. It has since trimmed its losses to trade at 100.65/68. \par \par \uc2  ** The euro fell to a record low of 59.52 pence against sterling on Wednesday, down nearly 18 percent from peaks it scaled on its first day of trading in January, 1999. \par \par \uc2  ** The Bank of England's trade-weighted effective exchange rate index for the euro is down about two percent since the ECB's last meeting. It is down about 15 percent since the euro's launch. \par \par \uc2  LATEST COMMENTS ON THE EURO BY POLICYMAKERS \par \par \uc2  * Federal Reserve Bank of New York President William McDonough said on Thursday the weakness of the euro had some European central bankers concerned but added it did not represent a problem from an economic point of view. \par \par \uc2  * Bundesbank chief Ernst Welteke said in an interview published on Wednesday that the euro would appreciate in time: \par \par \uc2  "What we see these days is more a question of the dollar's strength than weakness in the euro, which is largely due to more robust U.S. growth and the higher (interest) rates that go with it," he was quoted as saying. \par \par \uc2  "If you look at long-term developments through dollar/mark exchange rates, the dollar's rise at the moment is, moreover, totally coherent with what we've seen in the past. In 1985, the dollar was worth 3.42 (per mark). In 1995, it was worth no more than 1.36 - which does not mean I'm calling for the euro to continue weakening," he said. \par \par \uc2  * Belgian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Guy Quaden was quoted on March 24 as saying the ECB did not seek to maintain a precise rate of exchange for the euro but did not rule out possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the ECB to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 : \par \par \uc2  "There has never been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central banks to support the euro," he said. "But that measure exists and no one has ever said that the ECB would never use it." \par \par \uc2  He also said further political unity in Europe would support the euro. \par \par \uc2  "Hesitations related to fiscal harmonisation and the difficulties of agreeing on a European candidate for the IMF, for example, have not given the euro zone the strongest and most coherent image," Quaden said. \par \par \uc2  "I am among those who think that the long-term power of the euro zone and the euro itself is related to greater political cohesion," he said. \par \par \uc2  * ECB Vice President Christian Noyer said on March 23 he expected the euro to rally soon against the dollar and that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a tool available to the ECB: \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a tool in our hands...We may use this tool any time, as the U.S. would probably argue. It's not that we will never use it, otherwise we would not need any reserves." \par \par \uc2  Asked about the euro's rate against the dollar, Noyer said: "It's an important indicator. We monitor it, we have no target we have said, as such. But of course the developments, the level, are something that we follow extremely carefully." \par \par \uc2  * ECB Chief Economist Otmar Issing said on March 23 there were two main reasons for the current external weakness of the euro, one of which was the insufficient attractiveness of the euro area for direct investments: \par \par \uc2  "The negative balance of 147.2 billion euros in direct investments in 1999 speaks a clear language," Issing said. \par \par \uc2  * German consumer price inflation has accelerated significantly in recent months, driven in part by import prices rising due to a weak euro, the Bundesbank said in its March monthly report. \par \par \uc2  * ECB President Wim Duisenberg said on March 20 the euro's fall against the dollar had not led the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to contemplate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets: \par \par \uc2  "Although the ECB has the power to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, it has up until today not used that power at all," he said. \par \par \uc2  "The only \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have taken place in the course of last year were \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  made at the request of and on behalf of and on account of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The ECB itself has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and the development of the exchange rate - the exchange rate not being a direct target for us - has not given rise to us to contemplate doing anything like that," he said. \par \par \uc2  * Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke said on March 20 the ECB was concerned with the external value of the euro only in that it might affect inflation within the 11-nation euro area: \par \par \uc2  "The exchange rate is considered by the ECB in so far as it could impair the price outlook." \par \par \uc2  * ECB President Wim Duisenberg said on March 20 that the rise in ECB interest rates announced on March 16 was in no way linked to euro weakness: \par \par \uc2  "The answer is to no extent has it (the decision) been determined by developments of the exchange rate," Duisenberg told a European Parliament committee when asked if the timing of last week's decision had been linked to the euro. \par \par \uc2  ((Swaha Pattanaik, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3u039e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
