\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 :Dlr Down Vs Yen As Japan Long-Term Rates Rise \b0\par\par\uc2  By Takeshi Takeuchi \par \uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 07:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar recouped some of late Friday's losses against the yen on the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears, but fell again Monday in Asia as Japanese long-term interest rates rose. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also weakened against the euro on the back of a speculation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might soon tighten credit again. \par \par \uc2  The dollar started the new week in Asia in the mid-Y106 level to which it dropped at the close of Friday trading, amid rumors that the BOJ has started mulling a credit-tightening, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But Japanese interbank traders and overseas short-term investment funds soon started buying the dollar to cover short positions, dealers said. With Japan's current fiscal year closing on Thursday, traders feared that the BOJ might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar, so they bought dollars ahead of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying, dealers noted. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese monetary authorities don't want the dollar to significantly weaken against the yen from around current levels on the book-closing day," said Shinji Imanishi, senior trader at Sumitomo Trust and Banking. "Such notion has rekindled the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears, prompting traders to buy back dollars." \par \par \uc2  Any dollar fall against the yen hurts Japanese exporters' and investors' profits in yen terms. Market players believe the Japanese government wouldn't allow the dollar to go below Y105.00 before the fiscal year-end. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, however, met a new round of selling later Monday as the yield of Japan's benchmark 10-year government bond rose to 1.885%, up from 1.870% at Friday's close, dealers said. Fears of massive new issues of government bonds in the new fiscal year pushed up the yield, which in turn prompted some traders to sell dollars due to narrowing interest rate differentials between the U.S. and Japan, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT (12:15 a.m. EST), the dollar was quoted at Y106.80, below Y106.94 late Friday in New York. The dollar was at Y107.45 late Friday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $0.9782, higher than $0.9766 late Friday in New York. The euro was at $0.9750 in Tokyo Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders expect the dollar to be range-bound between Y106.00 and Y108.00 for the coming week. Japanese institutional investors' dollar-asset repatriation, by far the dominant dollar-negative force, has almost peaked out, while Japanese exporters are preventing the dollar from rising above Y108.00 by placing substantial sell-orders at mid-Y107 levels and above, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  However, market participants have no consensus on the dollar's direction against the yen after Japan's new fiscal year starts on April 1. \par \par \uc2  Some traders argue that the dollar is likely to strengthen because of Japanese institutional investors' fresh investment in U.S. assets in the new year. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar could rise to as high as Y110.00 since Japanese investors are likely to undertake new rounds of investment into dollar assets," said Takyuki Togawa, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Tokai Bank. \par \par \uc2  But others claim that the dollar may well drop below Y105.00 in the new fiscal year due to the expected improvement in the Bank of Japan's quarterly business sentiment survey, "tankan," scheduled to be released on April 3. \par \par \uc2  "The market has not yet fully factored the expected improvement into the trading," said Sumitomo Trust's Imanishi. "Besides, I think the dollar/yen is in the middle of long-running trend of depreciation," he noted. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y104.47, slightly up from Y104.44 in New York late Friday. The euro was at Y104.76 in Tokyo late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.5935, up from $1.5927 in New York and higher than $1.5878 in Tokyo Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Takeshi Takeuchi; 813-5255-2934; takeshi.takeuchi@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates which accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture which is updated hourly. \par \par \uc2  Subscribers can find this table: \par \par \uc2  -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087. \par \par \uc2  -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX \par \par \uc2  -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT \par \par \uc2  -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw3r01klg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
