\par\par \b \uc2  Euro,Swiss franc boosted by SNB hike, Noyer. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kanta Watanabe \par \uc2 931 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 03:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 24 (Reuters) - The euro and Swiss franc continued to gain on Friday morning in Tokyo after rallying overnight on the Swiss National Bank's decision to raise its target range for three-month interbank interest rates by a bigger-than-expected 75 basis points. \par \par \uc2  Concerns over possible actions by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the euro also prompted overseas fund operators to buy back euros, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Such expectations emerged after ECB Vice President Christian Noyer said on Thursday that he expected the euro to rally soon against the dollar and that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a tool available to the ECB. \par \par \uc2  At 0218 GMT, the euro was quoted at $0.9746/50 up from $0.9716 late on Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro at 104.69/78 yen versus 104.35 yen in New York. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc was at 1.6407/17 per dollar, versus 1.6451 in New York. \par \par \uc2  The SNB said on Thursday it was putting its range for the three-month Swiss franc London interbank offered rate (LIBOR) up to 2.5 to 3.5 percent, from 1.75 to 2.75 percent, citing unexpectedly strong economic growth and a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It's rather unusual to see the Swiss franc move this much during Tokyo trading hours," said a dealer at a Japanese commercial bank. \par \par \uc2  "As Japanese are not major players in Swiss/dollar, it may be overseas fund operators continuing to unwind their Swiss carry trade positions," he said. \par \par \uc2  Swiss franc carry trades, in which leveraged operators borrow low-cost Swiss franc funds to invest in higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , were losing attractiveness due to higher Swiss interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "There is talk of more stop-loss Swiss franc buy orders below 1.64. Those levels may be tested later in the day when the European markets open," said a dealer at a city bank. \par \par \uc2  But some players doubted that European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to rally against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Minoru Shioiri, deputy manager at Nippon Credit Bank, said: "It would be difficult for the euro to continue rising just based on expectations of higher European interest rates. This is because fears of inflation are stronger in the United States." \par \par \uc2  "The pace of credit tightening is still likely to be faster and the number of tightening steps greater in the United States than Europe," Shioiri added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar edged higher against the yen on weekend commercial dollar demand. It also drew indirect support from euro buying against the yen by U.S. fund operators, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But the greenback was capped by offers from Japanese exporters and wariness ahead of the release on April 3 of the Bank of Japan's quarterly "tankan" survey of business sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 107.43/51 yen compared with 107.32 yen in New York late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "It would be risky to build up yen short positions before the tankan, as we've been seeing positive surprises in Japanese economic data lately," said a dealer at a trust bank. "So I doubt the dollar could reach 109 yen before the tankan." \par \par \uc2  There was no immediate market reaction to comments by Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa that he was not aware of any request by French monetary authorities for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of France to support the euro. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa was referring a Jiji news agency report on Tuesday that the BOJ had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros versus the yen on March 8 at the French Finance Ministry's request. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's Policy Board is meeting on Friday, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely expected to keep its monetary policy unchanged. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0248 GMT.  All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   23 Mar.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab     0.9739   0.9716   +0.24    -3.29   1.0070\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab      107.47   107.32   +0.14    +5.15   102.21\par  Euro/yen\tab \tab     104.72   104.35   +0.35    +1.57   103.10\par  Dlr/swiss\tab \tab    1.6422   1.6451   -0.18    +3.27   1.5902\par  Stg/dlr\tab \tab      1.5879   1.5873   +0.04    -1.84   1.6176\par  Can/dlr\tab \tab      1.4672   1.4690   -0.12    +1.50   1.4455\par  Aus/dlr\tab \tab      0.6066   0.6052   +0.23    -7.57   0.6563\par  Euro/swiss\tab \tab   1.6004   1.5984   +0.13    -0.20   1.6036\par  Euro/stg\tab \tab     0.6133   0.6115   +0.29    -1.70   0.6239.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 0248 GMT.  All data taken from Reuters\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   23 Mar.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab     0.9739   0.9716   +0.24    -3.29   1.0070\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab 