\par\par \b \uc2  Miyazawa unaware of France asking BOJ to help euro. \b0\par\par\uc2 197 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 02:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 24 (Reuters) - Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Friday he was not aware of any request by the French monetary authorities for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of France to support the euro. \par \par \uc2  Jiji news agency reported on Tuesday that the BOJ had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the euro on March 8 at the request of the French Finance Ministry. \par \par \uc2  Pressed on whether he could deny that the French and their \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had made such a request to Japan, Miyazawa told reporters at a regular briefing: "I have not heard anything about this." \par \par \uc2  The Jiji report prompted much interest in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. It was denied by the French and analysts said they found it hard to believe France would go behind the back of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ask Japan to support the euro. \par \par \uc2  But some traders were not so quick to dismiss the story. \par \par \uc2  "We know that the MOF and the French Finance Ministry have quite close links," said one trader at a European bank on Wednesday. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3o03i3h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
