\par\par \b \uc2  Cold water poured on solo French euro defence tale. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 13:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers may be the source of many a tall tale but they found it hard to swallow a report on Tuesday that France bypassed the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and asked Japan to buy euro/yen on its behalf earlier in March. \par \par \uc2  The Jiji newswire story, citing an unnamed international financial source, referred to euro/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on March 8, which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had confirmed took place during European hours. \par \par \uc2  Traders have so far assumed the action had been part of Japan's solo efforts to curb industry-crippling yen strength which has seen it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen on at least 16 separate occasions since June 1999, usually for dollars. \par \par \uc2  They said they would stick to this view after French sources said it was inconceivable that Paris requested the March 8 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Japanese Finance Ministry, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the ECB all declined to comment on the Jiji report. \par \par \uc2  Traders said any solo French action would have been against the spirit, and probably the letter, of the regulations which govern how euro zone \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be conducted and therefore gave the Jiji story little credence. \par \par \uc2  "It is poppycock and doesn't really hold water," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader familiar with the activities of central banks. \par \par \uc2  "France just would not ask Japan to support the euro without going through the ECB and they would hardly be trying to weaken the yen." \par \par \uc2  FRANCE NOT SEEN STRIKING OUT ON ITS OWN \par \par \uc2  The theories which sprang up to explain the Jiji story ranged from suggestions that there had been a misunderstanding or mistranslation to the thesis that ordinary day-to-day transactions by France had been mistakenly refered to as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said they found it hard to believe France would launch \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  without the agreement of the ECB given such a move would be doomed to fail without the backing of other central banks. \par \par \uc2  "For France to strike out on its own separately from ECB and without its sanction would be an astounding and extraordinary development," said Graham Bishop, adviser, European financial affairs at Salomon Smith Barney in London. \par \par \uc2  "If they were going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it has been agreed for a long time that it would be in a time and manner that would have the most impact given the quantity of money available to them is so much smaller than the turnover in the foreign exchanges." \par \par \uc2  France was also not expected to take up a solo defence of the euro given it would spotlight schisms in European policy-making circles and prove even more damaging for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Central banks' past habit of declaring within hours whether they were conducting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of another authority also led traders and analysts to give the Jiji report less credence, said Johnathan Bailey, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Paribas in Paris. \par \par \uc2  This was, for example, the case in June 1999 when the ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros for yen on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  but made it clear soon after its appearance on the foreign exchanges that the action had not been on its own account. \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3l03uta\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
