\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro firms as risk of ECB hike seen growing. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 09:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 16 (Reuters) - The euro firmed across the board on Thursday, supported by expectations the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would deliver a rate hike after its fortnightly monetary policy meeting later in the day. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro managed to hold near the day's highs, more than two yen above record lows set earlier in the week but short of Wednesday's post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  highs above 103 yen, as caution prevailed before the ECB meeting. \par \par \uc2  "Eurozone data has been surprisingly strong recently and the probability we are going to see a rate hike is higher," said Alexander Nagel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is slightly firmer because today people are putting on trades, in case the ECB surprises with a 50 basis point rate hike. Twenty-five basis points is the consensus and 50 basis points is more likely than nothing." \par \par \uc2  The ECB raised its refinancing rate by 25 basis points to 3.25 percent on February 3. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose as far as $0.9706, up from $0.9672 in New York on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Stronger-than-expected German data, released earlier on Thursday, also supported rate hike expectations. \par \par \uc2  Wholesale prices rose 1.1 percent in February from January, and rose 5.7 percent on the year, above analysts' forecast of 0.6 percent and 5.1 percent rises respectively. \par \par \uc2  EURO REMAINS VULNERABLE \par \par \uc2  In Asia, traders said a bout of euro buying against the yen by Japanese banks sparked speculation the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may capitalise on a possible ECB tightening by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but European traders said this was an unlikely scenario. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, the euro settled about a quarter of a yen below the day's high of 102.65 yen, up from 101.73 in New York's close and from record lows of 100.52 set earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ, which has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least 16 times since June, 1999, to curb the yen's industry-crippling strength, bought about $5 to $6 billion worth of dollars for yen on Wednesday, traders in Tokyo said. \par \par \uc2  But on Wednesday, Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa flatly denied that Japan would ask for ECB assistance, saying that action in Tokyo alone had been effective. \par \par \uc2  Furthermore, analysts noted that the ECB had repeatedly ruled out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone. In December, ECB President Wim Duisenberg stated the bank would never \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the yen without the participation of the Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  Overall, sentiment against the euro remained bearish and traders said there was a risk that any ECB rate hike would not provide lasting support to the euro unless it was matched by hawkish comments from the ECB officials. \par \par \uc2  "There is more euro upside if you see a ECB rate hike," said Nagel from Dresdner Kleinwort Benson. \par \par \uc2  "But the trend is still downwards for the euro unless the ECB comments are surprisingly strong and they try to give some credibility to the euro, which has been one of the drawbacks." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, there was no help in sight for the dollar against the yen, despite lingering BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears, as there was no sign of the United States agreeing to weaken the yen, given that the U.S current account deficit continues to widen and the U.S. would not like Japan to become even more competitive, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was idling around mid-105 against the yen, little changed from New York's close of 105.63. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, U.S. trade data showed the current account deficit blew out to a record $99.8 billion in the fourth quarter. The full year shortfall ballooned 54 percent to $338 billion, or 3.8 percent of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  "I can just see the Treasury agreeing to push the yen lower so Japan can export more to the States," said a U.S. bank dealer in Tokyo, with more than a small trace of sarcasm. \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 8968, sabrina.ghani@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3g04k47\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
