\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED-FOCUS-Asia stocks mostly firmer, euro higher. \b0\par\par\uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 14:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  In SINGAPORE item headlined "FOCUS-Asia stocks mostly firmer, euro higher" please read in para 11...close 0.49 percent higher at 8,682.76...instead of...0.44 percent higher at 8,677.96 (corrects figures). \par \par \uc2  A corrected repetition follows. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 16 (Reuters) - Most Asian stock markets made modest gains on Thursday with Japan's Nikkei closing higher for the first time in four days and Taiwan's nervous market boosted as a stabilisation fund entered for the first time. \par \par \uc2  The euro won a rare lift as the market's imagination worked overtime ahead of a key European monetary policy meeting later in the day, but both the euro and the dollar remained short of Wednesday's highs after Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei index (.closed up 0.92 percent at 19,253.23 as major high-tech and internet stocks continued to rebond after falling sharply in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  "The market is still in the middle of recovery after recent sharp falls," said Kunihiro Hatae, equities general manager at Tokyo Securities. "We can tell which issue is still a market favourite and which is not by seeing how much lost ground they recover and how fast." \par \par \uc2  High-priced technology and Internet stocks had suffered a serious setback over the last few weeks, in line with falls in the United States and as profit-taking intensified in the run-up to the end-March corporate book closings. \par \par \uc2  Markets in Singapore, Kuala Lumpur and Jakarta were closed for holidays. \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG OFF, TAIWAN FUND ENTERS \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's benchmark index ended the morning slightly lower but defensive stocks rose as investors took a cue from the Dow's rebound amid uncertainity over Taiwan's presidential election. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average chalked up its fourth-biggest point rise on Wednesday, jumping 320.17 points, or 3.26 percent. The Nasdaq composite index, meanwhile, lost ground for its third straight session, off 2.63 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index ended the morning down 0.16 percent at 16,720.02. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan stocks rebounded at the close as state funds entered to support the market, reversing an early tumble triggered by China's fresh threats over Saturday's election. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's main TAIEX index had lost as much as 4.51 percent in early trade before state buying helped stocks close 0.49 percent higher at 8,682.76. \par \par \uc2  There was early panic selling after Chinese Premier Zhu Rongji warned at a Wednesday news conference that relations with Taiwan were at a crossroads, but officials confirmed that a new T$500 billion stock stabilisation fund entered the market for the first time to stem the tide. \par \par \uc2  KOREA ALSO ENDS UP \par \par \uc2  South Korea's benchmark index closed slightly higher as institutional selling limited gains led by a rebound in recently shunned blue chips, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Korea Composite Stock Price Index (KOSPI) closed up 0.44 percent at 841.22. \par \par \uc2  "Blue chips, after being hit hard for days, recovered on aggressive buying from foreign investors," said Kim Jeong-hwan, a market strategist at LG Investment and Securities. \par \par \uc2  Australian shares ended higher as investors dumped "new economy" stocks and returned to recently neglected sectors such as banks and mining. \par \par \uc2  EURO BENEFITS \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a listless session had been livened by a spate of conspiracy theories, the favourite being that the Bank of Japan was ready to capitalise on a possible rate hike from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive the euro up. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, the market was tense enough that the euro did rise on the dollar to $0.9691 from $0.9672. That was no help to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which was idling at 105.64 yen from 105.64 late in New York. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3g04dsq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
