\par\par \b \uc2  Point Of View:The Sound Of One  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervening \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Robert Flint \par \uc2 770 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 18:05\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't succeed in a long-term weakening of the yen through single-handed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, at least according to conventional wisdom. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ can only slow or smooth \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, but can't reverse market sentiment on its own, maintain virtually all market pundits. In testimony before Congress last year, no less than Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan stated, "Sterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  just does not work." \par \par \uc2  But what if the best and brightest are taking the wrong approach to the BOJ's actions? Many times blind monkeys and dart boards have outclassed the world's best experts on equities at picking winning stocks. Perhaps a less rational, more exotic, method could prove equally profitable when applied to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  How about using Zen, a branch of Buddhism practiced most widely in Japan? Adherents seek to obtain intuitive illumination of mind and spirit through meditation, especially on paradoxes. Because today's economy can't quite decide whether it is Old or New, paradoxes abound, making it very suitable target for Zen. \par \par \uc2  One of the tools used in Zen meditation is the koan, which is a statement, riddle or question which appears on the surface to be nonsensical or self-contradictory. Through contemplating the koan, the student of Zen gains insight into reality itself. \par \par \uc2  A classic koan runs: "You know the sound of two hands clapping. What is the sound of one hand clapping?" Redone for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the koan becomes: "You know the sound of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . What is the sound of one \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?" \par \par \uc2  Sometimes the koan contains the answer to highlight the paradox. An apt example would be: "When is a strong dollar not a strong dollar? When it is a strong dollar." Or given a slightly different setting: "When is a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  not a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? When it is the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Given the size and complexity of international financial markets, it's not surprising investors and commentators borrow religious and mystical terms to describe them. \par \par \uc2  This is particularly true of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where a mind-boggling $1.5 trillion changes hands every day. Billions upon billions in dollars, yen, euros and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are transferred between accounts across the globe almost instantaneously at the touch of a button. \par \par \uc2  Monetary officials, whether here in the U.S. or abroad, are said to "preach" to markets, with their words interpreted by financial "gurus." We hear about Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers repeating the strong-dollar "mantra" or about the "Delphi" utterances of Fed Chairman (and "oracle") Greenspan. \par \par \uc2  Of course, the major players - transnational banks and brokerage houses - tackle the market in a very rational manner. They employ topnotch economists and cutting-edge technology to track and analyze \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, churning out millions of words a day which appear on electronic screens or in faxed newsletters. \par \par \uc2  Corporations rely on a myriad of financial instruments to hedge against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuation, while speculators hope to earn big profits by taking big risks on the same gyrations. \par \par \uc2  But analytical models which require mainframe computers aren't always enough to explain markets, much less predict them accurately. References in obscure publications, even whispered gossip, still have a profound effect on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. High technology and sophisticated analysis haven't been able to do away with unconfirmed rumors and word-of-mouth as a force in markets. \par \par \uc2  It's this combination of the ultra-rational and irrational which can make \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets so puzzling. Individual investors, and even some of the big players, begin to feel like the caveman who watched a bolt of lightning split a tree and set it afire 40,000 years ago. Lacking the basis for understanding what they saw, our Cro-Magnon ancestors gave supernatural explanations to natural phenomena. \par \par \uc2  In a recent article, one well-known financial journalist went as far as to describe "the Zen of listening to Greenspan." This would imply that logical analysis isn't enough to understand the Fed Chairman's pronouncements, dose of intuitive insight is needed as well. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If this is true about Greenspan's words, why not the market as a\par whole? Maybe investors should put aside technical charts and tables of\par economic indicators to look elsewhere for real understanding. But where\par and how to look? Here a classic koan applies. When asked the best way to\par find enlightment, the Zen master replied: "That's simple. Do not seek\par it."\par -By Robert Flint, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4408\par email: robert.flint@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw3g01giu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
