\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asia markets rattled by U.S. falls. \b0\par\par\uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 08:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 15 (Reuters) - Most Asian stock markets moved lower on Wednesday, rattled by a four percent overnight dip in the U.S. Nasdaq index reflecting a plunge in U.S. biotechhnology stocks. \par \par \uc2  The dollar jumped against the yen in late trade in Tokyo after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, dealers said, taking the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back toward the 106 yen level after it had dropped below 105. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the share market, Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei 225 average\tab    ended down for the third day in a row as biotechnology and drug shares skidded in line with the overnight losses in their U.S. counterparts, and bank stocks also tumbled.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nikkei closed down 63.24 points or 0.33 percent at 19,078.60.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But core information technology stocks such as Sony Corp  and NTT Docomo  extended Tuesday's rebound after a prolonged sell-off triggered by investors aiming to cash in on earlier gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Sony's fundamental growth potential has not been changed at all despite recent price declines," said Tetsuya Ishijima, chief strategist at Okasan Securities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Issues like Sony and NTT Docomo will lead the Tokyo market higher towards the end of the business year", which runs through end-March in Japan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TAIWAN LOWER ON POLLS, TECH SALES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite active state fund buying that helped a short-lived early rebound, Taiwan stocks closed sharply lower, chilled by pre-election worries and the Nasdaq tumble.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The TAIEX\tab    closed down 195.55 points, or 2.21 percent, at 8,640.03 from Tuesday's 8,835.58 finish.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Several brokers said they expected strong pre-election support at around 8,400 and predicted the index would fluctuate between 8,400 and 9,000 before Saturday's presidential vote.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hong Kong stocks ended the morning down after a poor earnings report by telecom company SmarTone Telecommunications Holdings  compounded the effects of the sharp drop on the Nasdaq.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The benchmark Hang Seng index\tab  , which has fallen over five percent in the past two days after a global tumble in technology stocks, had dropped 0.97 percent by the break to 16,764.65 after falling as low as 16,553.17.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par KOREAN SHARES DOWN\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par South Korea's benchmark index closed lower as select telecom shares and biotechnology-related stocks fell.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Korea Composite Stock Price Index (KOSPI)\tab    closed down 1.09 percent at 837.53.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Pharmaceutical stocks were a poison pill. The weight of those shares grew towards the end of last year on global interest in biotech theme shares," said Jang Woo-jin, an analyst at Samsung Securities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Australian shares finished on a weaker note as investors moved out of heavy hitting media and telecommunications stocks and into banking issues, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The benchmark All Ordinaries Index\tab    closed down 23.9 points at 3,163.5, its third consecutive fall.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But Australia's flagship airline Qantas  flew skywards, closing up almost 10 percent on easing fears of tougher competition in the Australian aviation market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par DOLLAR JUMPS AFTER BOJ STEPS IN\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepped into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market when the dollar was trading around 104.80/90 yen. The dollar quickly leapt to a high of 105.75 yen, and the BOJ was still \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at that level in late Tokyo trade, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The BOJ last \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a week ago, selling yen for dollars in an effort to weaken the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which has appreciated 6.4 percent against the dollar since February 22.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3f04oou\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
