\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen recoups losses as BOJ concerns wane. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Deepthi Wickremasinghe \par \uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 14:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 15 (Reuters) - The yen rebounded from losses suffered earlier on Wednesday in the wake of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for dollars for the third time this year. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rallied to a high of 106.20 yen and the euro firmed to 103.25 yen in the wake of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action, which took place during Asian hours and was later confirmed by Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa. \par \par \uc2  While Miyazawa warned the foreign exchanges Japan could take more such action if necessary, the yen clawed back ground during European hours. Analysts said the absence of any further action so far and a feeling the BOJ was going against the tide was responsible for the retracement. \par \par \uc2  "Price action on the yen supports the view that there is still a lot of underlying demand for the yen and the BOJ will need repeated action if they want to stop the yen from appreciating beyond 104," said Michael Lewis, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Since the BOJ last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on March 8, the yen had appreciated 2.6 percent against the dollar. Its most recent boost has been since Monday when a surprisingly strong capital spending component of fourth quarter GDP data restored confidence that the economy was on a growth path. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said investment capital flows ahead of the Japanese fiscal year-end on March 31 would encourage strong demand for yen over the next couple of weeks. \par \par \uc2  "It does look like we will need more BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because there is just such a large wall of money coming into Japan ahead of the fiscal year-end," said Lewis at Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  Bankers in Tokyo spotted the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying dollars for yen when the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was languishing at 104.80/90 yen. That lifted the dollar to a high of 105.75 yen and the BOJ was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at that level as well. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen has pulled back more than a 1/2 yen from its post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  peak but was up around one percent from Tuesday's 104.55 six-week low. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was mildly effective but the market is fairly sceptical and if they don't come in again I suspect we will see dollar/yen retrace just about all the way," said Cameron Crise, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read in London. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ are totally going against the wind in the sense that there is good underlying demand for yen from Japanese investors to hedge some of their exposures." \par \par \uc2  EURO/YEN PULLBACK WEIGHS ON EURO/DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  Market disappointment on the lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity to follow up the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was putting pressure on euro/yen which had fallen 1-1/4 yen from its 103.25 day's peak, but was still around 1.5 percent above record lows around 100.50 set a day earlier. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The lower levels in euro/yen were keeping the European single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trapped below its earlier $0.9702 peak\tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The renewed risk of Japanese investors hedging their euro holdings is undermining euro/yen which is weighing on euro/dollar," said Deutsche Bank's Lewis. Looking ahead, he said the euro should be mildly supported if the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raises its 3.25 percent refinancing rate on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There are a lot of net short (positions) out there so it could cause a bit of a short term squeeze," Lewis said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, sterling was holding above Tuesday's $1.5677 seven-month low, helped in part by stronger-than-expected British average earnings data which rose 5.9 percent in the three months to January, its highest since July 1992.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Today's data will certainly maintain risk that the MPC (Monetary Policy Committee) may feel the compulsion to raise rates again, so sterling is reasonably well supported," said Crise at Warburg Dillon Read.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3f04mbh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
