\par\par \b \uc2  Options not seen key to BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  timing. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 15:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 15 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign exchanges on Wednesday has got traders intrigued as to how much influence \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  option expiries have on the timing of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's actions. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ bought dollars for yen just after 0600 GMT, when 105 strikes options, said to be worth about $1 billion, expired. \par \par \uc2  This sparked talk that the BOJ, which has already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  once earlier this month and at least 16 times since June 1999, was trying to maximise the impact of its action by avoiding running into option-related sales of dollars aimed at keeping spot as close as possible to the strike price. \par \par \uc2  But traders said tracking options expiries would not be a foolproof way of guessing when the BOJ would next \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "At the moment the market is very thin and if there are large amount of options coming off and the spot is near it, the BOJ may take it into consideration," said an options trader at a European bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "But that sort of thing does not happen every day and on the rare occasion it does happen, it's reasonable for them to take it into account. But there is no way they are going to be looking at daily expiries if they are going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  BOJ KEEPS WATCH ON OPTIONS ACTIVITY \par \par \uc2  Talk that the BOJ did take options expiries into account in deciding on the timing of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday was given credence as traders said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept a close eye on options activity. \par \par \uc2  However, they added that this was not going to be the only factor which would determine the timing of any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  happened at around 0615 GMT and it took the dollar up by about 3/4 yen from around 104.80. It was right after the options expiries, so you may argue that they had something to do with it," said a options traders for a European bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "While I am sure that the BOJ is aware of most aspects (of options) - they often inquire about risk reversals (directional preference reflected by option prices) - they are more interested in the nature of spot market rather than option strikes." \par \par \uc2  There were reportedly another batch of 105 strike options expiring on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  This was not expected to have an impact on the timing of any further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but risked countering the impact of BOJ action by acting as a magnet, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think that they'll have that much impact on when and where they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The key thing is they don't want rapid yen appreciation because it doesn't give Japanese time to hedge therefore the BOJ acts to stem the tide," said Robert Howard, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options analyst at Thomson Global Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the yen's speedy rebound from lows set after the BOJ action left traders sceptical about how effectively \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could stem yen's gains. If anything, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen adding to the gyrations in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was hovering around mid-105\tab   at 1216 GMT, down from a post-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  high of 106.20, but up about a yen from day's lows of 104.70.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders' scepticism was reflected in one-month dollar/yen implied volatilities which remained lofty at above 13 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "They tend to take it up a yen or so and then New York and London takes it back down," said the options trader at the European Bank in London.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I suspect the dollar will be back to 105 - the market is yet to be convinced and people don't want to sell volatilities."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((London Capital Markets, + 44 171 542 8968, sabrina.ghani@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3f04gsu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
