\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Strong yen holds gains against euro, dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 15:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 14 (Reuters) - The yen sailed to a record high against the euro and hit six-week peaks against the dollar as investors expressed new hope that Japan's economic recovery is finally picking up speed. \par \par \uc2  "The yen is trading up across the board and today we see more of the same story. While there are no new factors per se, fundamentally investors are becoming much less pessimistic than they were one month ago," said Brown Brothers Harriman \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Anne Parker Mills. \par \par \uc2  Ignoring soft growth data for the October-December period that effectively put Japan back into recession, the market focused instead on the sharp increase in capital spending and Japanese officials' optimistic comments about the future. \par \par \uc2  Also, analysts said Japanese investors' practice of bringing funds home before the fiscal year ends on March 31 had helped underpin the yen. \par \par \uc2  Overnight the yen drove the euro to 100.79 yen per euro, its lowest level ever, while the dollar slipped to 104.72 yen, its lowest since Jan. 28. \par \par \uc2  Both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had steadied slightly as New York trading got underway, with the euro trading at 100.94 yen and the dollar trading at 104.81 yen. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro failed to capitalise on Monday's brief gains against the dollar above $0.97 and spent the session mired in a tight range below $0.9650. \par \par \uc2  "The consistently poor performance of the euro will lead euro/yen lower," said Tony Norfield, head of global treasury research at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  News that U.S. February retail sales rose a stronger than expected 1.1 percent, topping Wall Street's forecast for a 1.0 percent rise, could prove supportive for Wall Street, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "If this helps stocks that could help the dollar and hurt the euro," BBH's Mills said, adding that if the euro were to fall below 95.90 cents, the market's focus may shift from a strong yen back to a weak euro story. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no clear sign yet that the market was bottoming out and they suggested that euro/yen may remain especially vulnerable. \par \par \uc2  Rising speculation the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may need to hike interest rates again, moving them up either a quarter point or even a half point - possibly as soon as this week - has failed to boost the euro because U.S. rates are also expected to be boosted. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile some nervousness remains about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially after Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa warned that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would act again if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market movements were rapid. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan sold yen last week and officials had often said publicly they worried that a strong yen could choke off the economy's recovery. \par \par \uc2  While the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a factor, dealers said it was not longer the top talking point. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile broad-based yen strength triggered a drop in sterling/yen to 2-1/2-month lows around 164.50. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the fall in sterling/yen in part helped fuel a fall in the pound to $1.5683, its lowest since late July 1999. It has since recovered some to $1.5718. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said expectations that interest rate differentials between Britain and other major economies, which had been sterling-supportive, might lose their beneficial function were also putting pressure on the pound. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England left its key repo rate unchanged at 6.0 percent at its March meeting. The U.S. Federal Open Market Committee is widely expected to hike U.S. interest rates next week and speculation is growing that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise interest rates at its upcoming governing council meeting on Thursday. \par \par \uc2      ((--Svea Herbst-Bayliss, U.S. Financial Markets Desk, \par \par \uc2  212-859-1639 )) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 9:27 a.m. (1427 GMT). All data taken from \par \par \uc2  Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close \par \par \uc2  at 4:30 p.m. (2130 GMT). \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab    Last     US Close  %Chg   YTD %    1999\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab March 13\tab \tab \tab Close\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr\tab \tab    0.9655   0.9645   +0.10   -4.12   1.0070\par Dlr/yen\tab \tab     104.89   105.61   -0.68   +2.62   102.21\par Euro/yen\tab \tab    101.29   101.87   -0.57   -1.76   103.10\par Dlr/swiss\tab \tab   1.6682   1.6700   -0.11   +4.91   1.5902\par Stg/dlr\tab \tab     1.5720   1.5792   -0.46   -2.82   1.6176\par Can/dlr\tab \tab     1.4612   1.4608   +0.03   +1.09   1.4455\par Aus/dlr\tab \tab     0.6145   0.6162   -0.28   -6.37   0.6563\par Euro/swiss\tab \tab  1.6108   1.6105   +0.02   +0.45   1.6036\par Euro/stg\tab \tab    0.6136   0.6110   +0.43   -1.65   0.6239\par      All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news ...\tab     Debt...   Indicators...\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\tab \tab \tab \tab \par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \par \par All spots\tab \par Tokyo spots\tab \par Europe spots\tab \par Volati