\par\par \b \uc2  Yen wary of BOJ pretenders, but mood upbeat. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 833 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 03:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 14 (Reuters) - The yen eased back from overnight highs on Tuesday as some in the market bet the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow its rise. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a U.S. investment bank had bought up dollar/yen presumably in the hope the BOJ would act to lift the dollar and so give them a profitable rally to sell into. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, some suspected the bank was trying to spoof a nervous market - make it look like the BOJ had stepped in and so generate the rally themselves. \par \par \uc2  If so, the ploy had not worked well with the dollar topping out at 106.00 yen before retreating to 105.67 by 0225 GMT. That was still up on the overnight trough of 104.80 but a yen away from Monday's local high at 105.70. \par \par \uc2  The move was mirrored in euro/yen which inched up 102.00 yen before relaxing to 101.83, leaving it in the middle of Monday's 101.40-102.50 range. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar the euro had recoiled to $0.9644 from a $0.9730 high overnight. \par \par \uc2  "The thing is that the BOJ has built these expectations, so the market goes long of dollars, and if they don't then turn up, those longs are cut and the dollar falls," said a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "In some ways \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is creating the very problem it's supposed to solve," he added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also argued the authorities were making the task all the harder by so energetically talking up the economy. \par \par \uc2  After a chorus of upbeat comments about October-December GDP data on Monday, Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa picked up the melody this morning saying the figures suggested Japan had started a self-sustaining recovery. \par \par \uc2  Given such confidence it was hard for the MOF to then justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by claiming the yen's rise was a threat to recovery, traders said. \par \par \uc2  And Miyazawa did seem to recognise the contradiction when he tied possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to "rapid" moves in the yen. \par \par \uc2  "They can't use the economy argument anymore, so they've switched to speed as the reason for action," said a dealer at a second U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  "But that means the market can still take the yen higher as long as it's in an orderly manner," he concluded. \par \par \uc2  Which was why most expected support at 105.40 yen to give way for another test of 104.80 in the short term. The bullish view on the yen was encouraged by an equally bearish view on the euro, which was producing heavy pressure on the euro/yen cross. \par \par \uc2  Traders suspect some Japanese institutions are desperate to cover their euro exposure having unwound hedges or actually bought into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it was rallying from 106.00 to 112.00 yen in late February, only to see it collapse to a lifetime low around 101.95 last week. \par \par \uc2  And since the euro's problems were largely home-grown there was little the BOJ could do about it, they said. \par \par \uc2  "The rumour is the BOJ had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on euro/yen on its own last week because the ECB actually declined to act on their behalf," said the U.S. bank dealer, noting it was rare for one \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to refuse a request from another. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Wim Duisenberg did voice some concerns about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, saying a weakening or volatile euro was not in Europe's interests. \par \par \uc2  However, traders argued that was not the same as calling for a "strong" euro. Indeed, Duisenberg emphasised that he was not so concerned about a particular exchange rate level rather that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  be stable. \par \par \uc2  They noted the euro had ben unable to hold onto its rally above 97 cents overnight, even though the trend-setting Nasdaq index ended 2.8 percent lower. \par \par \uc2  "It only took selling from a single hedge fund to knock it off 97 cents," said the dealer. "Sentiment is still not going it's way." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0225 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   13 Mar.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab     0.9644   0.9645   -0.01    -4.23   1.0070\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab      105.67   105.61   +0.06    +3.39   102.21\par  Euro/yen\tab \tab     101.83   101.87   -0.04    -1.23   103.10\par  Dlr/swiss\tab \tab    1.6690   1.6700   -0.06    +4.96   1.5902\par  Stg/dlr\tab \tab      1.5771   1.5792   -0.13    -2.50   1.6176\par  Can/dlr\tab \tab      1.4610   1.4608   +0.01    +1.07   1.4455\par  Aus/dlr\tab \tab      0.6154   0.6162   -0.13    -6.23   0.6563\par  Euro/swiss\tab \tab   1.6108   1.6105   +0.02    +0.45   1.6036\par  Euro/stg\tab \tab     0.6110   0.6110   +0.00    -2.07   0.6239.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3e04kop\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
