\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar regains some ground in U.S. trading. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Genevieve Wilkinson \par \uc2 871 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 19:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 13 (Reuters) - The dollar regained its composure on Monday, paring heavy overnight losses against the yen and euro as Wall Street quickly bounced back from an early morning sell-off. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded at 105.45 yen, trimming its overnight losses from 1.3 percent to just under three-quarters of a percent shortly after noon. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the dollar erased nearly all of its overnight loss of three-quarters of a percent to trade near 96.30 cents per euro. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had come under pressure overnight amid a global decline in technology stocks which analysts feared would infect the U.S. Nasdaq index, whose recent record gains had been instrumental in underpinning the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The decline sent Japan's benchmark Nikkei tumbling 2.84 percent on Monday. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks did tumble at the opening bell with the Nasdaq index losing more than 4 percent within the first minutes of trading, but they quickly trimmed their losses. \par \par \uc2  At midday, the Dow Jones industrial average was down 7 points, or .07 percent, while the Nasdaq was down 98 points, or almost 2 percent as investors parked their money in the strongest of world stock markets amid a global unloading. \par \par \uc2  "(The bounce-back) has been positive for the dollar," said Chris Widness, senior international economist with Chase Securities in New York. "But going forward I think it's still going to be a rough week for the dollar because we have a whole slew of economic numbers that could suggest the (Federal Reserve) has a lot to do." \par \par \uc2  February retail sales are released on Tuesday, the Producer Price Index (PPI) on Thursday and the Consumer Price Index (CPI) on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "If the numbers suggest higher U.S. inflation, obviously that's bad for the dollar," Widness said, adding that even if the CPI and PPI figures were benign, spiraling oil prices could still boost inflation. \par \par \uc2  EARLY LOSSES HELP YEN \par \par \uc2  With dealers clearly worried about the fate of Wall Street, the Nikkei's sharp drop plus worse than expected economic data failed to push the yen much lower. \par \par \uc2  Instead investors focused on Japanese officials' upbeat assessment of the economic outlook which overshadowed the larger-than-expected drop of 1.4 percent in Japan's gross domestic product between October and December 1999. \par \par \uc2  Also, the "mere fact that private capital spending rose 4.6 (percent) was making the yen pretty attractive," said Dennis Heidt, chief trader at Paribas in New York. \par \par \uc2  Japan is now technically in recession again but officials promised better times ahead. \par \par \uc2  Ichizo Ohara, a senior official in Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party, said the pace of growth in the first half of this year may leave the Bank of Japan no choice but to end its zero rate policy, in which it has kept interest rates near zero percent in order to stimulate domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  His comments buoyed the yen and had all the more impact because of his previously dovish comments on monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Also the yen received a boost as investors sought to bring funds back to Japan to adjust their books before the current fiscal year ends on March 31. \par \par \uc2  But the yen's gains are somewhat muted against expectations that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may yet again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rapid appreciation. \par \par \uc2  "There are still concerns that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  given how quickly the yen rose in the wake of its GDP report," Widness said. \par \par \uc2  The BoJ last week \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice, buying dollars and euros for yen though its intended effect to stem the yen's quick rise was short-lived \par \par \uc2  Traders said there was also more interest in the cross rates including euro/yen, hovering near the session's low at 101.65 and the Swiss franc, which firmed against the dollar at $1.66. \par \par \uc2  ((-U.S. Financial Markets Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 12:56 P.M. (1756 GMT).  All data \par \par \uc2  taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the \par \par \uc2  daily U.S. close at 4:30 P.M. (2130 GMT). \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab    Last     US Close  %Chg   YTD %   1999\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab 10 Mar.\tab \tab \tab Close\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr\tab \tab    0.9627   0.9628   -0.01   -4.40   1.0070\par Dlr/yen\tab \tab     105.44   106.23   -0.74   +3.16   102.21\par Euro/yen\tab \tab    101.45   102.24   -0.77   -1.60   103.10\par Dlr/swiss\tab \tab   1.6723   1.6710   +0.08   +5.16   1.5902\par Stg/dlr\tab \tab     1.5764   1.5759   +0.03   -2.55   1.6176\par Can/dlr\tab \tab     1.4610   1.4576   +0.23   +1.07   1.4455\par Aus/dlr\tab \tab     0.6155   0.6146   +0.15   -6.22   0.6563\par Euro/swiss\tab \tab  1.6104   1.6085   +0.12   +0.42   1.6036\par Euro/stg\tab \tab    0.6099   0.6104   -0.08   -2.24   0.6239\par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news ...\tab     Debt...   Indicators...\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2