\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hurt as high tech stocks seen slipping. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 973 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 15:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW YORK, March 13 (Reuters) - The dollar fell on Monday, hitting six-week lows against the yen\tab   and 11-day lows against the euro      as dealers feared a global downdraft in technology stocks may infect the U.S. Nasdaq index.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There was widespread concern that foreign investors who had been instrumental in the technology-laden Nasdaq's recent record-beating rally could start withdrawing their money and sell dollars in the process, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Japanese investors in particular were seen as a potential linchpin for the dollar amid signs they were seeking to take profits on their holdings of technology stocks home before the end of their financial year at the end of March. \par \par \uc2  "The concern about the technology stocks is the main reason that the dollar is weaker across the board this morning," said Klaus Kusber, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  News the Chicago Mercantile Exchange's June Nasdaq 100 index futures fell sharply, hitting their initial limit down in early trading, accentuated the markets' concerns and pulled the dollar down further. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq index fell at the open and was off more than 4 percent in early trade. \par \par \uc2  Shedding more than one percent of its value against the yen, the dollar fell below 105 yen in early New York trading, reaching fresh six-week lows. \par \par \uc2  Similarly, the dollar slipped nearly one percent to trade at 97.18 cents to the euro and was off about three-quarters of a percent against the Swiss franc at 1.6567. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These losses came after Japan's benchmark Nikkei\tab    fell 2.84 percent on Monday with technology stocks hit hard as domestic investors cashed in gains before their end-March book closing.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The dollar was the biggest beneficiary of the rally in high-technology stocks in the recent weeks and months because it helped attract the capital needed to finance the current account deficit. But these stocks got hammered in Asia and if we now see the Nasdaq start to fall, the dollar would be hard hit," Dresdner's Kusber added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts said the dollar would also be at risk if a sustained pullback in the Nasdaq reduced the perceived likelihood of a U.S. interest rate increase at next week's Federal Open Market Committee meeting.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the same time domestic factors also boosted the yen and the euro against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese officials' upbeat assessments of the economic outlook overshadowed a larger-than-expected drop of 1.4 percent in Japan's gross domestic product between October and December 1999 which pushed the economy into a technical recession.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders also chose to focus on the report's good news; Dennis Heidt, chief dealer at Paribas in New York, said "the mere fact that private capital spending rose 4.6 was making the yen pretty attractive."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Promising better times ahead, a senior official in Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party, Ichizo Ohara, said the pace of growth in the first half of this year may leave the Bank of Japan no choice but to end its zero rate policy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par His comments buoyed the yen and had all the more impact because of his previously dovish comments on monetary policy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the yen's gains may slow, especially as dealers worry about the prospect of more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , one week after the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We could see a battle heating up between the central banks and the speculators and it could all come to a head this week," Heidt added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, the dollar's woes against the euro were compounded as higher rates looked even more imminent in the euro zone in the wake of comments by European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ECB President Wim Duisenberg was quoted on Monday as saying euro weakness was a concern because of its potential inflation impact. ECB Vice President Christian Noyer was reported over the weekend as saying euro weakness was contributing to risks to price stability.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said the euro was also supported by signs that Italy's Fiat was set to unveil a strategic alliance with the world's biggest carmaker, General Motors , which was expected to involve a partial cross-shareholding.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Also Texas Utilities  announced a takeover bid for Spain's Hidroelectrica del Cantabrico , a deal which analysts estimated could be worth up to 2.3 billion euros.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said speculation such cross-border deals could generate demand for euros was proving supportive for the euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     ((--Svea Herbst-Bayliss, U.S. Financial Markets Desk,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 212-859-1639 ))\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 9:26 a.m. (1426 GMT). All data taken\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par close at 4:30 p.m. (2130 GMT).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab    Last     US 